\caption{Upper convex hull of $(k,\log_{10}a_k)$
for the degree-five example. A segment of slope
$\sigma$ gives a tropical root $10^{-\sigma}$;
its horizontal length gives the multiplicity.
The red points lie below the hull or inside a hull segment.}
\label{fig:tropical-degree-five}
\end{figure}

We return to the degree-five polynomial with coefficient norms
\[
    (a_0,\ldots,a_5)=(1,10,10^4,10^5,10^6,10^4).
\]
Its interior hull vertices are $j=2$ and $j=4$, with
dominance intervals
\[
    I_2=[10^{-2},10^{-1}],
    \qquad
    I_4=[10^{-1},10^2].
\]
The midpoint scales and width parameters are
\[
    \gamma_2=10^{-3/2},\qquad U_2=10^{1/2},
    \qquad
    \gamma_4=10^{1/2},\qquad U_4=10^{3/2}.
\]
For the scaled polynomials
\[
    Q_j(t)=\frac{P(\gamma_jt)}{a_j\gamma_j^j},
\]
the logarithms of the coefficient norms are
\begin{align*}
    \bigl(\log_{10}\|Q_{2,k}\|_2\bigr)_{k=0}^{5}
    &=\left(-1,-\frac32,0,-\frac12,-1,-\frac92\right),\\
    \bigl(\log_{10}\|Q_{4,k}\|_2\bigr)_{k=0}^{5}
    &=\left(-8,-\frac{13}{2},-3,-\frac32,0,-\frac32\right).
\end{align*}
In both cases, the dominant coefficient has norm one.
The decay bound becomes
\[
    \log_{10}\|Q_{j,k}\|_2
    \leq -|k-j|\log_{10}U_j.
\]
Figure~\ref{fig:tropical-coefficient-decay} compares
the coefficient heights with this bound. The wider
dominance interval for $j=4$ gives a larger $U_j$
and hence a steeper decay bound.



